<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Rescue for somebody who is badly hurt but still walking.

    Vanilla refuses this twice, and both refusals are keyed to Downed.
    HealthAIUtility.WantsToBeRescued returns false for a pawn who is not downed, which is what
    removes FloatMenuOptionProvider_RescuePawn's option; and JobDriver_TakeToBed fails the job
    outright, with a FailOn that reads "job def is Rescue or Capture, and the takee is not downed".

    That second check is why this def exists instead of a patch. It tests the job def by identity,
    so a job that is neither Rescue nor Capture walks past it untouched. Everything else in
    JobDriver_TakeToBed is already general: the prisoner branches read job.def.makeTargetPrisoner,
    which is false here, and TakeeRescued is true for any humanlike who is not being arrested, so
    the pawn is tucked into the bed as a patient exactly as a rescue would leave them. The driver
    is therefore reused as it ships, and no part of it is patched or reimplemented.

    Fields copied from vanilla's own Rescue def, since that is the behaviour being extended rather
    than replaced. The report string is the one deliberate difference: this is not a rescue in
    vanilla's sense and saying "rescuing" in the pawn's job bar would claim it was.

    Referenced from C# as OrdersDefOf.Gideon_RescueImpaired.
  -->
  <JobDef>
    <defName>Gideon_RescueImpaired</defName>
    <driverClass>JobDriver_TakeToBed</driverClass>
    <reportString>carrying TargetA to bed.</reportString>
    <casualInterruptible>false</casualInterruptible>
    <carryThingAfterJob>true</carryThingAfterJob>
    <dropThingBeforeJob>false</dropThingBeforeJob>
  </JobDef>

</Defs>
